---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import MedalBadge from '../../components/MedalBadge.astro';

const allSystems = await getCollection('systems');

// Define the x86 family systems
const systemX86Slugs = [
  'ibm-pc-dos', 'tandy-1000', 'fujitsu-fm-towns'
];

const systems = systemX86Slugs
  .map(slug => allSystems.find(s => s.slug === slug))
  .filter(Boolean)
  .filter(s => s.data.status !== 'vault')
  .sort((a, b) => {
    const tierOrder = { platinum: 0, gold: 1, silver: 2, bronze: 3 };
    const tierA = tierOrder[a.data.medal_tier] ?? 4;
    const tierB = tierOrder[b.data.medal_tier] ?? 4;
    if (tierA !== tierB) return tierA - tierB;
    return (b.data.total_lessons || 0) - (a.data.total_lessons || 0);
  });

const totalLessons = systems.reduce((sum, s) => sum + (s.data.total_lessons || 0), 0);
const totalGames = systems.reduce((sum, s) => sum + (s.data.total_games || 0), 0);

// Group by medal tier
const platinum = systems.filter(s => s.data.medal_tier === 'platinum');
const gold = systems.filter(s => s.data.medal_tier === 'gold');
const silver = systems.filter(s => s.data.medal_tier === 'silver');
const bronze = systems.filter(s => s.data.medal_tier === 'bronze');
---

<Layout title="x86 Architecture Track | Code Like It's 198x">
  <main class="min-h-screen bg-gradient-to-b from-gray-900 to-black text-green-400">
    <div class="container mx-auto px-4 py-12">
      <header class="mb-12">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-4 mb-4">
          <a href="/tracks/" class="text-gray-400 hover:text-green-400 transition">
            ← Back to Tracks
          </a>
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-center gap-6">
          <div class="text-6xl">🔹</div>
          <div>
            <h1 class="text-5xl font-bold mb-2 text-transparent bg-clip-text bg-gradient-to-r from-yellow-400 to-amber-400">
              Intel x86 Family
            </h1>
            <p class="text-xl text-gray-300">
              The architecture that became the PC standard
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </header>

      <div class="grid md:grid-cols-4 gap-6 mb-12">
        <div class="bg-gray-800 rounded-lg p-6 border-2 border-gray-700">
          <div class="text-3xl font-bold text-white">{systems.length}</div>
          <div class="text-gray-400">Systems</div>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-gray-800 rounded-lg p-6 border-2 border-gray-700">
          <div class="text-3xl font-bold text-white">{totalLessons.toLocaleString()}</div>
          <div class="text-gray-400">Total Lessons</div>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-gray-800 rounded-lg p-6 border-2 border-gray-700">
          <div class="text-3xl font-bold text-white">{totalGames.toLocaleString()}</div>
          <div class="text-gray-400">Games to Build</div>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-gray-800 rounded-lg p-6 border-2 border-gray-700">
          <div class="text-3xl font-bold text-white">1981-1995</div>
          <div class="text-gray-400">Era Covered</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-gray-800 rounded-lg p-8 mb-12 border-2 border-gray-700">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4 text-white">About the x86 Architecture</h2>
        <div class="prose prose-invert max-w-none text-gray-300">
          <p class="mb-4">
            The Intel x86 architecture, beginning with the 8086/8088 processors, established the foundation 
            for modern personal computing. Despite its complex segmented memory model and irregular instruction 
            set, x86's IBM PC compatibility and continuous evolution made it the dominant PC architecture.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-2 text-green-400">Key Characteristics</h3>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 mb-4">
            <li><strong>Segmented memory:</strong> 16-bit segments with 16-bit offsets for 1MB addressing</li>
            <li><strong>CISC architecture:</strong> Complex instructions that can do multiple operations</li>
            <li><strong>Register scarcity:</strong> Limited general-purpose registers requiring careful management</li>
            <li><strong>Backward compatibility:</strong> Each generation maintains compatibility with previous</li>
            <li><strong>Real and Protected modes:</strong> Evolution from 8086 real mode to 286/386 protected mode</li>
            <li><strong>x87 FPU:</strong> Optional floating-point coprocessor for mathematical operations</li>
          </ul>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-2 text-green-400">Register Architecture (8086/8088)</h3>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 mb-4">
            <li><strong>General registers:</strong> AX, BX, CX, DX (16-bit, split into 8-bit halves)</li>
            <li><strong>Index registers:</strong> SI (source), DI (destination)</li>
            <li><strong>Pointer registers:</strong> SP (stack), BP (base pointer)</li>
            <li><strong>Segment registers:</strong> CS (code), DS (data), ES (extra), SS (stack)</li>
            <li><strong>Instruction pointer:</strong> IP for program counter</li>
            <li><strong>Flags register:</strong> Status and control flags</li>
          </ul>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-2 text-green-400">Evolution Through the 80s-90s</h3>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
            <li><strong>8088:</strong> 8-bit external bus version used in original IBM PC</li>
            <li><strong>80286:</strong> Protected mode, 16MB address space (IBM AT)</li>
            <li><strong>80386:</strong> 32-bit architecture, flat memory model, virtual memory</li>
            <li><strong>80486:</strong> Integrated FPU and cache, pipelining</li>
            <li><strong>Pentium:</strong> Superscalar execution, dual pipelines</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="mb-12">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mb-6 text-white">Learning Path</h2>
        
        {gold.length > 0 && (
          <div class="mb-8">
            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
              <span>🥇</span>
              <span class="text-gray-300">Gold Tier - Advanced Study (2,048 lessons each)</span>
            </h3>
            <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
              {gold.map(system => (
                <a href={`/systems/${system.slug}/`} class="block bg-gray-900 rounded-lg p-6 border-2 border-gray-800 hover:border-green-400 transition-all">
                  <div class="flex items-start justify-between mb-3">
                    <h4 class="text-lg font-bold text-white">{system.data.name || system.data.title}</h4>
                    <MedalBadge tier={system.data.medal_tier} size="small" />
                  </div>
                  <p class="text-sm text-gray-400 mb-3">{system.data.year} • {system.data.manufacturer}</p>
                  <div class="flex gap-4 text-xs text-gray-500">
                    <span>{system.data.total_lessons} lessons</span>
                    <span>{system.data.total_games} games</span>
                    <span>{system.data.estimated_duration}</span>
                  </div>
                </a>
              ))}
            </div>
          </div>
        )}

        {silver.length > 0 && (
          <div class="mb-8">
            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
              <span>🥈</span>
              <span class="text-gray-300">Silver Tier - Solid Foundation (1,024 lessons each)</span>
            </h3>
            <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
              {silver.map(system => (
                <a href={`/systems/${system.slug}/`} class="block bg-gray-900 rounded-lg p-6 border-2 border-gray-800 hover:border-green-400 transition-all">
                  <div class="flex items-start justify-between mb-3">
                    <h4 class="text-lg font-bold text-white">{system.data.name || system.data.title}</h4>
                    <MedalBadge tier={system.data.medal_tier} size="small" />
                  </div>
                  <p class="text-sm text-gray-400 mb-3">{system.data.year} • {system.data.manufacturer}</p>
                  <div class="flex gap-4 text-xs text-gray-500">
                    <span>{system.data.total_lessons} lessons</span>
                    <span>{system.data.total_games} games</span>
                  </div>
                </a>
              ))}
            </div>
          </div>
        )}
      </div>

      <div class="bg-gradient-to-r from-yellow-900 to-amber-900 rounded-lg p-8 border-2 border-yellow-700">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4 text-white">Recommended Learning Path</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4 text-gray-300">
          <div class="flex gap-4">
            <div class="text-2xl">1️⃣</div>
            <div>
              <h3 class="font-semibold text-white">Start with IBM PC and DOS</h3>
              <p class="text-sm">Learn the fundamentals of x86 assembly, BIOS interrupts, and DOS programming. Master Mode 13h graphics and understand the PC architecture that defined an era.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="flex gap-4">
            <div class="text-2xl">2️⃣</div>
            <div>
              <h3 class="font-semibold text-white">Explore Enhanced Graphics with Tandy</h3>
              <p class="text-sm">Discover the Tandy 1000's enhanced graphics and sound capabilities. Learn how manufacturers extended the basic PC architecture for better gaming experiences.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="flex gap-4">
            <div class="text-2xl">3️⃣</div>
            <div>
              <h3 class="font-semibold text-white">Master Multimedia with FM Towns</h3>
              <p class="text-sm">Experience Japan's take on PC gaming with CD-ROM, high-resolution graphics, and advanced sound synthesis on 386-based hardware.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-gray-800 rounded-lg p-8 mt-8 border-2 border-gray-700">
        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold mb-4 text-white">Why Learn x86 Assembly?</h3>
        <div class="text-gray-300 space-y-3">
          <p>
            While x86 assembly is more complex than 6502 or Z80, understanding it provides insight into 
            how modern computers evolved. The techniques for dealing with segmented memory, optimizing for 
            limited registers, and working with PC hardware directly translate to understanding modern systems.
          </p>
          <p>
            The IBM PC's open architecture spawned countless clones and innovations, making it the most 
            important platform in computing history. Learning to program it at the assembly level reveals 
            the foundations of modern computing.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>
</Layout>